(
  '88888888-8888-4888-8888-888888888888',
  'blog',
  'turkish-meze-guide',
  'Turkish Meze Guide: How to Build a Shared Table',
  'Learn how creamy, bright, smoky, spicy and fresh Turkish meze work together at a shared table.',
  '[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Meze turn ordering into a shared experience. Small dishes arrive together, allowing each person to combine flavors and move through the meal at a comfortable pace. Across Turkish cooking, meze can feature vegetables, yogurt, legumes, herbs, seafood, cheese, grains and olive oil. The exact selection varies by region, season and kitchen.</p><h2>Think in contrasts, not quantity</h2><p>A balanced meze order does not need every dish on the menu. Begin by combining different temperatures, textures and flavor profiles. A creamy dip can sit beside a crisp salad. A smoky eggplant preparation can balance a bright tomato or pepper dish. Pickles or lemon can refresh the palate between richer bites.</p><h3>Creamy and cooling</h3><p>Yogurt-based dishes and hummus bring body to the table. Their mild richness can soften heat and pair naturally with bread. Some preparations include garlic, herbs, tahini or olive oil, so guests with ingredient concerns should ask the team about the current recipe.</p><h3>Vegetable-forward and bright</h3><p>Eggplant, peppers, tomatoes, cucumbers, greens and legumes create variety without making the table feel heavy. Roasting can develop sweetness and smoke; fresh herbs and acidity can keep a dish lively. Turkish cooking offers many vegetable preparations, but availability is specific to the current menu.</p><h2>Use bread with intention</h2><p>Bread is both part of the meal and a way to experience the texture of a meze. Tear a manageable piece, pair it with one or two flavors, and return to another dish. Ordering bread gradually can help the table keep space for warm plates.</p><h2>Move from meze to the rest of the meal</h2><p>Meze can be the beginning of dinner or the center of a lighter meal. For a fuller table, follow with pide, a grilled plate, vegetables, rice or bulgur. The transition works best when the later dishes add something new rather than repeating the same flavor profile.</p><h2>Example combinations</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Table mood</th><th>Starting approach</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fresh and light</td><td>Vegetable dishes, herbs, acidity and bread</td></tr><tr><td>Rich and comforting</td><td>Yogurt, legumes, roasted vegetables and a warm plate</td></tr><tr><td>Broad introduction</td><td>One creamy, one bright and one smoky or spicy meze</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Explore the <a href=\"/menu\">Turquaz menu</a> to see the current selection, then <a href=\"/#reservation\">reserve a table</a> if you are planning a shared meal in San Francisco.</p>"}]',
  'published',
