(
  '99999999-9999-4999-8999-999999999999',
  'blog',
  'pide-vs-lahmacun',
  'Pide vs. Lahmacun: Two Turkish Oven Traditions',
  'Compare the shape, dough, toppings, texture and serving style of Turkish pide and lahmacun.',
  '[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Pide and lahmacun are both baked Turkish specialties, but they create different eating experiences. The easiest distinction is visual: pide is generally shaped like a long, open boat, while lahmacun is thin, round and covered edge to edge with a finely chopped savory topping. Their dough, texture and serving styles also differ.</p><h2>What is Turkish pide?</h2><p>Pide uses a shaped dough with raised edges that hold its toppings. The center can remain tender while the rim develops color and structure in the oven. Toppings vary and may include cheese, vegetables, egg or seasoned meat. The result is substantial enough to anchor a meal but works especially well when several varieties are sliced and shared.</p><p>The word pide can refer to different breads and regional preparations in Turkey, so one restaurant version should not be treated as the only definition. At a restaurant, the menu description is the best guide to its size and toppings.</p><h2>What is lahmacun?</h2><p>Lahmacun begins with a thinner round of dough and a finely distributed topping commonly built from minced meat, tomato, pepper, herbs and seasoning. It bakes quickly and develops a crisp, flexible texture. Diners often add fresh herbs, vegetables or lemon and then fold or roll each portion.</p><p>Although lahmacun is sometimes casually described as Turkish pizza, that shorthand misses its thinner construction, finely spread topping and customary accompaniments. It is useful as an introduction, not a complete definition.</p><h2>How texture changes the meal</h2><p>Pide offers a stronger contrast between its shaped crust and filled center. Lahmacun distributes flavor in a thinner layer and is often brightened at the table. Pide may feel like a centerpiece, while lahmacun can work as a starter, snack or shared component alongside meze and salad.</p><h2>Which one should you order?</h2><ul><li>Choose pide when you want a thicker, shaped bread with distinct toppings.</li><li>Choose lahmacun when you want something thin, crisp and easy to brighten with herbs or lemon.</li><li>Order both for the table when you want to compare two oven traditions directly.</li></ul><p>Selections can change, so check the <a href=\"/menu\">live Turquaz menu</a> rather than assuming a particular topping is available. Pair either option with meze or salad, and <a href=\"/#reservation\">reserve ahead</a> when organizing a group meal.</p>"}]',
  'published',
