  'aaaaaaaa-aaaa-4aaa-8aaa-aaaaaaaaaaaa',
  'blog',
  'turkish-desserts-baklava-guide',
  'Turkish Desserts and Baklava: A Practical Guide',
  'Explore baklava, pastry, nuts, syrup and the role of Turkish coffee or tea at the end of a meal.',
  '[{"type":"html","html":"<p>Turkish desserts range from crisp layered pastries to milk-based sweets, fruit preserves and confections. Baklava is the most widely recognized example, but even baklava is not one fixed recipe. The nuts, shape, syrup, size and regional style can all vary.</p><h2>How baklava gets its texture</h2><p>Baklava relies on many thin layers of dough, fat, nuts and syrup. Good texture comes from maintaining separation between the layers during baking and balancing crisp pastry with the added syrup. Pistachios and walnuts are common, though the nut depends on the style and kitchen.</p><p>The pastry is rich, so a small piece can provide a complete finish to a meal. Its sweetness also pairs naturally with a drink that is not itself heavily sweetened.</p><h2>Why Turkish coffee works with dessert</h2><p>Turkish coffee is made from very finely ground coffee and served unfiltered in a small cup. Its concentration and roasted character contrast with syrup and pastry. Sip slowly and allow the grounds to remain at the bottom of the cup.</p><p>Turkish tea offers a different pairing. It can feel lighter and extend the table after the food is finished. Guests can adjust sweetness according to preference.</p><h2>Beyond one famous pastry</h2><p>Turkish dessert traditions also include milk puddings, semolina-based sweets, fruit and pumpkin desserts, lokum, cookies and other pastries. Restaurants select from this broad tradition according to their kitchen, season and service. The current menu is the reliable source for what is available on a given visit.</p><h2>How to share dessert</h2><ol><li>Choose one dessert for every two or three guests when the table wants only a taste.</li><li>Add coffee or tea and compare how each changes the sweetness.</li><li>Ask about nuts and other allergens before ordering.</li><li>Take time with the final course rather than treating it as an afterthought.</li></ol><h2>Plan the full meal</h2><p>If dessert matters to your visit, avoid over-ordering at the start. A progression from meze to a warm dish and then pastry creates contrast without requiring a very large portion at any stage. Browse the <a href=\"/menu\">current menu</a> for available sweets and drinks. For breakfast, dinner or a gathering with friends, <a href=\"/#reservation\">reserve a table at Turquaz</a> in San Francisco.</p>"}]',
  'published',
